## F. Mathematical Verification

1. **Property Preservation:**
   - Referential transparency
   - Type safety
   - Mathematical properties
   - Performance characteristics
   - State isolation

2. **LiquidHaskell Requirements:**
   - For implementation details and patterns, refer to [liquidhaskell-verification.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/liquidhaskell-verification.mdc)
   - Core Type Refinements:
     ```haskell
     {-@ type NonNegative a = {v:a | v >= 0} @-}
     {-@ type Positive a = {v:a | v > 0} @-}
     {-@ type Percentage = {v:Double | 0.0 <= v && v <= 1.0} @-}
     {-@ type Bounded a low high = {v:a | low <= v && v <= high} @-}
     ```
   - Collection Constraints:
     ```haskell
     {-@ type SizedList a n = {v:[a] | len v == n} @-}
     {-@ type NonEmptyList a = {v:[a] | len v > 0} @-}
     {-@ type SortedList a = {v:[a] | isSorted v} @-}
     ```
   - Function Properties:
     ```haskell
     {-@ measure size @-}
     {-@ preserve :: (a -> b) -> a -> {v:b | preserve v == preserve a} @-}
     {-@ monotonic :: (Ord a, Ord b) => (a -> b) -> a -> a -> Bool @-}
     ```

3. **Verification Requirements:**
   - All core calculations must have refinement types
   - Operations must preserve domain constraints
   - Classification operations must be statically verified
   - Data structure operations must maintain invariants
   - Transformations must preserve conservation properties
   - Decimal precision must be statically guaranteed
   - No runtime checks for properties verifiable at compile-time
